1. General Development Rules

    Use Flutter best practices:

        Follow Clean Architecture (separate UI, business logic, and data layers).

        Use state management like Riverpod, Provider, or Bloc.

        Implement responsive design that adapts to all screen sizes and orientations.

        Keep components reusable and modular.

    Ensure null safety and proper type annotations.

    Use async/await for asynchronous operations; avoid blocking the UI thread.

    Comment your code meaningfully for maintainability.

    Follow Google Dart Style Guide.

2. UI & Design Rules

    Use Material 3 (Material You) or Cupertino style depending on platform.

    Maintain consistent spacing, padding, and margins (8px multiples).

    Font Family: Use a clean, professional font like "Inter", "Roboto", or "SF Pro Display".

    Use a primary color palette with:

        Primary: Eye-catching but professional (e.g., deep blue, teal, or purple)

        Accent: A complementary highlight color

        Neutral grays for text and backgrounds

    Add elevation, shadows, rounded corners (8–16px) for modern feel.

    Ensure accessibility (sufficient color contrast, readable text sizes).

    Add smooth animations for navigation and widget transitions.

3. UX Interaction Rules

    Provide clear navigation (bottom nav bar or side drawer as appropriate).

    Use meaningful icons from flutter_icons or Material Icons.

    Always provide feedback (loading indicators, success messages, error messages).

    Use input validation for all forms.

    Avoid clutter — keep screens focused on their main action.

4. Project Structure

lib/
  main.dart
  core/        # Constants, themes, utilities
  data/        # Models, API services, repositories
  features/    # Feature-specific UI, logic, and widgets
  widgets/     # Reusable widgets
  state/       # State management files
assets/
  fonts/
  images/

5. Example Deliverable Expectations

    Include:

        Properly structured pubspec.yaml with dependencies

        theme.dart file defining colors, typography, and styles

        Screen files with clean widget trees

        Responsive layouts using LayoutBuilder or MediaQuery

        Animations for page transitions and key elements

